ituations  and more specifically the people that we  close to people that we can trust and he  proposes that language in fact evolved  as a means of keeping track of  reputations keeping track of who's the  good guy who's the bad guy who you  should avoid who you should not engage  with because they might be liars  cheaters murderers and so on so he's  basically saying that language evolved  for the purpose of gossip  so gossip being the idea that as a  community we keep track of people's  reputations and I think nowadays  reputations are more important than ever  or they're more relevant than ever if we  think of the ways in which information  now can be spread so it's not just word  of mouth or you whispering to somebody  that you know in the same way as we  otherwise might but now of course we all  have that megaphone of social media  these kind of and these pieces of  information can spread in no time and  they can be quite destructive so it's  it's very much a reality it's not just a  story that we tell ourselves thank you  see man right  I agree with Simone that morality is not  just a story we tell ourselves though I  do agree we do tell lots of stories to  ourselves and lots of our stories about  our own motivations might be fabricated  but our morality is not just about the  social fabric either and here's where I  might part ways with Simone it  transcends both the social and it  transcends the individual so morality is  the point at which we get to think about  someone other than ourselves we get to  think we get to escape the prison of the  self and think about what's going on in  other people and what matters to them  and although hilary mentioned that  morality is different all over the world  and there are many different moral  practices first of all we need to  distinguish what people take morality to  be and what morality is so what we take  morality to be is one thing and  sometimes that might be mistaken and  what morality is might be another and 